        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>What does a walking pad app do?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Usually some mix of remote control, logging each walk, preset programmes, virtual routes or games, and sync to other platforms. Logging is the feature most people keep using. The pad's calorie and step figures remain estimates whatever the app.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Do walking pads work with Zwift?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Some do. Look for a listing that names Zwift or the Bluetooth FTMS standard; the SereneLife on this page names Zwift and Kinomap. Otherwise a foot pod on your shoe can feed Zwift while you control the pad by hand. Most walking pads top out around 4 mph, so they suit Zwift walking.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Can a walking pad sync with Apple Health?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Some maker apps do. The MERACH UltraWalk W60's listing says its app syncs to Apple Health and Google Fit. For other pads, check the app's store page or settings. You can also record an indoor walk on an Apple Watch with any pad.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is there a walking pad with iFit?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              We did not find one. iFit runs on NordicTrack and ProForm treadmills with built-in screens. Walking pads use their makers' apps instead, and a few support third-party apps such as Zwift and Kinomap.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Why does my walking pad count different steps from my watch?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              The pad estimates steps from belt speed or a sensor; a wrist tracker counts arm swing, which drops when your hands are on a keyboard. Neither is exact. An ankle or shoe-mounted tracker counts better at a desk.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Do walking pad apps need a subscription?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              The maker apps on the pads here are described as free or come with the pad. Third-party platforms such as Zwift and Kinomap charge their own subscriptions. Check the app's store page for current terms.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

